# The command-line adapter

The command line is the clearest place to see where interpretation ends and effects
begin. It has no open buffers, persistent views, or background refreshes. It receives
an operation and an optional project root, captures saved input, calls the shared
semantic core, and reports one result.

That apparent simplicity is useful discipline. Argument handling must not read a
project merely to explain help. Inspection must not touch output paths. Checking
must not become generation by accident. Generation must not write until the entire
project and every destination have passed their respective checks.

## One lifecycle with an explicit write boundary

The adapter follows one ordered journey. Early exits are part of the design, not
special cases added around the main operation.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Args[Arguments] --> Usage{Valid request?}
    Usage -->|No| UsageError[Report usage error]
    Usage -->|Help| Help[Report help]
    Usage -->|Run| Discover[Discover saved documents]
    Discover --> Load[Load declared inputs]
    Load --> Interpret[Interpret one snapshot]
    Interpret --> Sound{Diagnostics empty?}
    Sound -->|No| Diagnose[Report diagnostics]
    Sound -->|Yes| Mode{Requested operation}
    Mode -->|Inspect| Inspect[Report structure]
    Mode -->|Check| Compare[Compare planned bytes]
    Mode -->|Generate| Write[Commit changed outputs]
```

The downward arrows mean that each stage earns permission for the next. The three
final branches share captured input and semantics, but only one crosses the write
boundary. Specialized publication and self-verification commands use the same
principle: they are explicit operations and do not silently run as a side effect of
ordinary inspection or generation.

## Arguments are resolved before project access

A command should be understandable even when the current directory is not a
Literate project. Help therefore completes before filesystem discovery. Unknown
commands, duplicate options, missing option values, and extra positional arguments
are usage errors rather than guesses about what the caller intended.

This distinction is important for automation. A malformed request is different
from a well-formed check that finds an invalid project. The terminal status and
structured response preserve that difference, allowing a script to decide whether
to fix its invocation or report a project problem.

The selected root is resolved once and must identify a directory. An empty discovery
is an error. Treating “no documents” as a successful project would make a wrong path
look reassuringly current.

## Discovery captures saved literate documents

Discovery walks beneath the selected root and selects regular files whose names
identify literate documents. Directory entries are sorted so that incidental
filesystem enumeration order cannot leak into the captured input. Known output,
dependency, and vendor directories are excluded under the same policy used by the
editor.

Symbolic links are skipped during input discovery. Following them would blur the
project boundary and could make the snapshot depend on content outside the selected
root. A selected document is checked again before reading so a path that disappears
or changes kind during discovery is not silently accepted as another file.

Discovery order is not yet semantic document order. The engine still places the
root index first, follows its declared chapter order, and then includes remaining
documents deterministically. The adapter finds candidates; the core decides their
meaningful order.

## Load only dependencies the project declares

Templates present a small acquisition loop. Their names occur in literate source,
so the adapter first gives the captured documents to dependency discovery. It then
loads only the normalized template paths returned by that pure step and supplies
the resulting texts to full interpretation.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant CLI as Command line
    participant Core as Semantic core
    participant Disk

    CLI->>Disk: Discover and read literate documents
    CLI->>Core: Ask for declared template paths
    Core-->>CLI: Safe normalized paths
    CLI->>Disk: Read available declared templates
    CLI->>Core: Interpret complete captured snapshot
    Core-->>CLI: Diagnostics, inspection, and plan
```

A missing declared template is omitted from the supplied template map so that the
engine can report the problem at the declaration that requested it. Permission
failures and other unexpected I/O errors are not disguised as absence; they remain
adapter errors. This gives source mistakes useful locations without hiding failures
in the environment.

The command line reads saved files. It cannot promise that its snapshot matches
unsaved editor buffers. Running disk-based checks while an editor has unpublished
changes asks two hosts to reason about different inputs, even though they share the
same semantics.

## Inspect describes structure without testing outputs

Inspection answers questions such as:

- Which documents belong to the ordered project?
- Which templates are referenced?
- Where is each fragment defined or extended?
- Which fragments does each contribution reference?
- Which roots claim output destinations?

The answer comes from parser metadata, including useful partial metadata when later
validation fails. Inspection is therefore not implemented as a separate pattern
search over source text. It sees the same declarations and source positions as the
engine.

Inspection deliberately stops before output preflight or comparison. A broken,
inaccessible, or missing output directory should not prevent a reader from asking
where a fragment is defined. Read-only is stronger here than “does not write”: the
operation does not even inspect destinations that are irrelevant to its question.

## Check compares content without changing it

Check asks whether the complete valid generation plan agrees with disk. It first
requires successful project validation; an invalid project has no trustworthy plan
to compare. Every planned destination is then inspected under the host filesystem
policy, and existing regular files are read for a byte comparison.

Bytes, not modification times, determine the result. A timestamp can change while
content stays equal, or remain misleading after content changes. Byte equality
also preserves the generator's claim that identical input yields identical output.

Each planned output receives one of three read-only observations:

- **current** means a regular file exists with exactly the planned bytes;
- **missing** means no entry exists at the destination beneath otherwise acceptable
  parents;
- **stale** means a regular file exists with different bytes.

Any missing or stale output makes the check unsuccessful, but no destination is
created or replaced. Unplanned old files are not inferred from the current plan and
are not deleted. Removing or renaming an output remains an explicit review task.

## Generate writes only the differences

Generation begins with the same complete plan and comparison as check. The adapter
passes that complete plan to the shared output transaction coordinator rather than
writing files independently. The coordinator compares bytes again and omits current
outputs from the changed batch. This avoids needless writes, preserves metadata
where no change is needed, and prevents avoidable editor or build-system events.

The coordinator
preflights every target, stages new bytes, preserves originals for recovery, and
reports whether failure occurred before commitment, during rollback, or after a
successful commit while cleaning up.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Plan[Complete valid plan] --> Inspect[Inspect every destination]
    Inspect --> Compare[Compare existing bytes]
    Compare --> Changes{Any changed outputs?}
    Changes -->|No| Current[Report all current]
    Changes -->|Yes| Batch[Commit the changed outputs as one batch]
    Batch --> Done[Mark completed writes]
```

A status becomes **written** only after the transaction reports that the requested
bytes were installed. Planned status must not be mistaken for physical success. If
cleanup fails after commitment, reporting must still say that the files were
committed while separately identifying retained recovery material.

## Filesystem policy belongs to the adapter

The semantic core rejects unsafe and colliding relative destinations before a plan
exists. The command-line adapter adds facts available only from the local
filesystem. It validates the trusted root and walks every existing path component
without following symbolic links. Ancestors must be directories; an existing target
must be a regular file; special files and directories at file destinations are
errors.

Genuine absence is expected for a new output. Permission errors, provider failures,
and malformed path states are not absence. Making this distinction prevents a
failed inspection from granting permission to create or replace something.

For changed local files, the adapter can carry ordinary permission bits from the
original to its staged replacement. It does not claim to preserve ownership,
access-control lists, or metadata that the transaction protocol cannot represent.
That limitation belongs in the result and documentation, not behind a broad promise
that “the file is unchanged except for content.”

## Reporting serves people and tools

Human output favors concise messages and source positions counted in the way people
normally read an editor. Diagnostics name the source path, line, column, and reason.
Progress and failures use the appropriate terminal stream so generated data can be
redirected without mixing it with explanations.

Structured output emits one complete object, including for usage and I/O failures.
It contains the requested command, overall success, diagnostics, errors, and output
statuses, with command-specific inspection or verification details when relevant.
Coordinates remain in the engine's zero-based convention so another tool can use
them directly.

Exactly one structured result is easier to consume than a stream of partially
related events. It also makes an important invariant visible: an output status
records an observed comparison or a completed write, never an optimistic intention.

Terminal completion falls into three broad classes:

- the request completed successfully;
- the request was valid but the project, environment, comparison, or verification
  failed;
- the invocation itself was invalid.

Keeping these classes distinct gives both a person and an automated caller enough
information to choose the next action without parsing prose.

## The adapter remains deliberately thin

“Thin” does not mean trivial. Discovery, path inspection, byte comparison, terminal
protocols, cancellation, and filesystem adaptation all require careful policy.
Thin means that none of those responsibilities redefine fragments, expansion,
document ordering, or diagnostics already owned by the semantic core.

The command line therefore exposes the architecture in a compact form: capture a
saved snapshot, interpret it once, stop early for read-only intent, and cross the
write boundary only with a complete valid batch. The next chapter follows that
batch across the boundary and examines what “safely” can honestly mean when several
filesystem entries must change.
